#!/usr/bin/python3
# WynlandOS - persistd: what a live stick keeps from one boot to the next.
#
# A live boot runs from RAM (boot/boot.c copies the system off the stick),
# so everything written was gone at the next boot: the account, the Wi-Fi
# networks, the browser's cookies, the programs leaf installed. persistd
# keeps them in the stick's data area -- partition 4, type 0x7D, from
# 1 GiB (tools/livepack.c; past any image Rufus writes, so a new image
# leaves it alone) up to the end of the stick or 4 GiB (/dev/usb0's
# offsets are 32-bit), grown to that on the first boot.
#
#   persistd restore    at boot, before the login screen (wynrc "persist")
#   persistd daemon     saves every 10 minutes when something changed, and
#                       when asked: control socket "\0persistd" -- "save",
#                       "status" (poweroff and reboot ask first, apps/power)
#   persist save|status the same, from a terminal
#
# What is kept: /home (but every ~/.cache), the accounts made on it (merged
# into the image's /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/group: a newer image's
# system accounts stay), Wi-Fi networks, the login screen's looks, and
# leaf's packages with their files.
#
# The area: two header sectors (the newer valid one counts: a sector torn
# by a pulled stick leaves the other), then archives -- tar, zstd. A save
# writes its archive where the current one is not and only then a header:
# the stick pulled in the middle of a save keeps the last one.
#
